{% comment %} Third width: 267x480. A narrow board beside something wider. {% endcomment %}

{% assign board = transit %}

<div class="screen dense">
  <div class="title-bar" style="padding: 14px 14px 8px">
    <div class="title truncate" style="font-size: 17px">{% if extension.values.heading != blank %}{{ extension.values.heading | truncate: 14 }}{% else %}{{ board.origin | truncate: 14 }}{% endif %}</div>
  </div>

  {% comment %}
    How many departures the box will actually hold.

    The fetch already trims to the "Departures to show" setting, so this is the
    other half of the question: whether the room exists to draw them. A board
    at half width is 480px tall and a board at half width in a nine-row slot is
    360px, and a fixed count clips the last row halfway through in the second -
    which reads as a rendering fault rather than as a board that is full.

    Chrome first, because an alert line and a notice strip each take a row's
    worth away from the list. A filter cannot appear inside an `if`, so every
    step is assigned.
  {% endcomment %}
  {% assign chrome = 44 %}
  {% if board.alert != blank %}{% assign chrome = chrome | plus: 40 %}{% endif %}
  {% if notices.size > 0 %}{% assign chrome = chrome | plus: 34 %}{% endif %}
  {% assign room = shape.height | minus: chrome %}
  {% assign fits = room | divided_by: 56 %}
  {% if fits < 1 %}{% assign fits = 1 %}{% endif %}

  {% if board.empty %}
    <div class="content layout layout--col layout--center" style="gap: 6px">
      <span class="i i-train i-md"></span>
      <p class="t-xs t-bold">None</p>
    </div>
  {% else %}
    <div class="content content--tight">
      <div class="list">
        {% for train in board.departures limit: fits %}
          <div class="list-item" style="padding: 8px 0">
            <span style="min-width: 0">
              <span class="t-sm t-bold" style="display: block">{{ train.line }}</span>
              <span class="t-xs truncate" style="display: block">{{ train.direction }}</span>
            </span>
            <span class="t-mono t-bold t-sm">
              {% if train.cancelled %}—{% else %}{{ train.expected }}{% endif %}
            </span>
          </div>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
    </div>
  {% endif %}

  {% comment %}
    Where another extension gets to say something on this screen. Glyphs like
    dashboard warning lights, with the loudest one's words beside them when the
    shape is wide enough to carry them.
  {% endcomment %}
  {% if notices.size > 0 %}
    <div class="notice-strip">
      {% for notice in notices %}
        {% if notice.overflow %}
          <span class="notice notice--more">+{{ notice.overflow }}</span>
        {% else %}
          <span class="notice notice--{{ notice.level }}" title="{{ notice.text }}">
            <span class="i i-{{ notice.icon }}"></span>
          </span>
        {% endif %}
      {% endfor %}

      {% if shape.wide and notices[0].text != blank %}
        <span class="notice-said">{{ notices[0].text }}</span>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  {% endif %}
</div>
